# Runtime lock files — flock targets, never source.  bin/heavy-job's default lock
# lives outside the repo (/tmp/heavy-job.lock), but a HEAVY_JOB_LOCK, a bench lock
# or a tool can point one into the tree, and a lock is transient by definition.
# NOTE: git has NO inline comments — text after a pattern is PART of the pattern,
# so each reason must sit on its own line above its rule.
*.lock

# ---- Per-box state ----------------------------------------------------------
# This repo serves two machines (Intel x86_64 and ASUS arm64).  The CODE is
# identical on both and stays tracked, and so do the per-box DECLARATIONS the code
# reads — config/hosts.d/*.conf, systemd/* — because a declaration is reviewed
# text, not a measurement.  What stays OUT of the remote is anything true of ONE
# machine only: a measurement taken on one card, that box's WSL host settings, and
# any run output.  Each box regenerates its own (`model scan`, the autotune's KV
# probe, the WSL config copied to C:\Users\<you>\.wslconfig).
#
# A rule here cannot untrack a file that is already in the index — `git rm
# --cached <path>` is the separate step, and `git ls-files -i -c` lists what a rule
# covers only if it is tracked.
#
# This box's .wslconfig values (memory=48GB, processors=16); the arm64 box differs.
config/wslconfig
# The KV-quant certification rows measured on THIS card at THIS ctx.  A box without
# it treats every kv type as uncertified and refuses the sweep — fail-closed.
config/kv-recall-baseline.tsv
# Run output.  logs/ is ignored above; this rule names the reason for the tree.
logs/

